Simple laceration from stepping on sharp object to plantar surface of foot
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Plantar cutaneous defect, Pain upon weight-bearing, Barefoot stepping mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-020
Reason for Visit: Acute, painful puncture wound on the sole of the foot requiring assessment for retained foreign body and deep tissue infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a barefoot puncture injury resulting in sharp pain and inability to bear weight. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to thoroughly probe the wound for retained foreign material, evaluate structural integrity, and initiate preventative care to avoid deep plantar space infection before morning clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I stepped on a piece of broken glass barefoot, and now I have a sharp pain in the bottom of my foot when I walk.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a clear history of walking barefoot indoors and stepping on a sharp object. This resulted in a focal puncture and small linear laceration on the thick, hyperkeratotic weight-bearing aspect of the sole. The patient reports sharp, acute pain precisely triggered by stepping and transferring body weight. There is minimal external bleeding, as the thick natural skin retracted over the narrow entry wound. Focal tenderness directly over the entry site is exacerbated by direct palpation.
Functional Status: Ambulating with a pronounced limp, avoiding pressure on the forefoot.
Social/Safety Concerns: Requires sweeping/vacuuming of the incident area to prevent secondary injuries.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 124/80 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, comfortable when seated, mild distress when forced to bear weight on the affected foot.
Physical Exam: Plantar examination reveals a focal cutaneous defect in the epidermis. There is pain upon weight-bearing. Visual inspection and careful bedside probing with a blunt instrument under good lighting reveal no visible or palpable foreign material in the tract. Normal capillary refill is present in all distal toes. Active and passive flexion of the digits is intact and pain-free, indicating an uninjured flexor apparatus. There is an absence of rapidly spreading erythema or significant plantar swelling in the acute post-injury phase.
Environmental Observations: Broken drinking glass identified in the kitchen; patient advised on safe cleanup.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Simple laceration/puncture to plantar surface of foot - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Retained foreign body (glass) - Ruled out due to: Thorough visual and tactile exploration of the wound tract yielding negative results; however, radiopaque imaging is definitively required if pain persists.

Flexor tendon laceration - Ruled out due to: Intact, strong, and pain-free active flexion of the toes against resistance.

Deep plantar space infection - Ruled out due to: Acute nature of the injury (<2 hours) with a complete absence of massive foot swelling, fluctuance, or systemic fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

A plantar puncture wound from broken glass inherently raises a high clinical suspicion for retained foreign material and deep structural damage. Bedside clinical evaluation successfully cleared the flexor tendons and performed initial tactile exploration of the wound tract. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to execute high-pressure localized irrigation, confirm the absence of immediate structural deficits, and implement strict non-weight-bearing protocols, ensuring the wound remained uninfected and un-traumatized until plain radiographs could be obtained the following day.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered local anesthesia to facilitate thorough high-pressure syringe irrigation of the wound tract with sterile saline. Applied a non-adherent dressing. Advised formal plain radiographs of the foot tomorrow to definitively rule out retained radiopaque glass shards. Tetanus prophylaxis updated.
Positive Behavioral Change: Utilize crutches or heel-walking to completely offload the puncture site for 48 hours. Preventing mechanical pressure stops the wound edges from rubbing together, preventing severe pain and allowing the deep dermal layers to fuse effectively.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Crutches (if available/required for offloading).
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 24-48 hours with imaging results (x-ray) to confirm complete absence of foreign bodies.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department if the foot becomes red, hot, swollen, excessively painful, or if you develop a fever and chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Glass is notoriously difficult to fully visualize clinically; an x-ray is the only definitive method to clear radiopaque fragments.
Clinical Course: The sharp pain upon standing will persist for 2-3 days. The thick skin of the sole heals slowly over 1-2 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if a red, tender lump develops under the skin, or if you notice red streaks traveling up your ankle.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Plantar wounds easily trap bacteria deep in the foot. Thorough washing and keeping weight off the foot prevents these bacteria from being driven further into the tissues.
Actionable Steps: Do not walk barefoot. Get an x-ray of your foot tomorrow as requested. Keep the dressing clean and completely dry.

